<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<wml>

  <card id="report" title="Grouped Sales Report">

    <!--
      WapForm for Web — two-level grouped sales report example (salesperson → customer → month)

      The query here is exactly the same as crosstab.wml (the same SQL, the same
      group by totals, the same date range and eno/cno filters); only the presentation
      changes from a "row x column matrix" to a "grouped list report". The customer column deliberately uses the raw Cno
      code (not converted to the Cshort short name), so the numbers in the two examples can be
      compared cell by cell: the cross-cell amount for a salesperson in a given month in crosstab.wml should match
      the detail row in this report for the same salesperson, customer code and month.

      <report> uses an engine completely different from, and much simpler than, <crosstab>: it relies on <group>
      tags + a switch called MyEnable to decide whether it is now producing a "group header", a "detail" or
      a "group subtotal", without computing any grid coordinates.

      This file uses two levels of <group> (two group change="..."), following
      the rules read from mywap.pas's CheckGroupHeaders/ParseGroupHeaders/ParseRecord/
      CheckGroupFooters/ParseGroupFooters:

        <report dataset="xy">
          ... report prefix (ParsePrefix), runs once ...
          <group change="first-level key, e.g. salesperson">
            ... before the nested <group> = first-level header, printed once when the first-level key changes ...
            <group change="second-level key, e.g. customer">
              ... before the nested <group> = second-level header, printed once when the second-level key changes ...
              <group change="innermost key, usually the detail's own unique key">
                ... detail row, printed once per record ...
              </group>
              ... after the nested <group> = second-level subtotal, printed once before moving to the next
                  second-level key ...
            </group>
            ... after the nested <group> = first-level subtotal, printed once before moving to the next
                first-level key ...
          </group>
          ... report suffix (ParseSuffix), runs once ...
        </report>

      N levels of grouping (headers + subtotals) need N+1 nested <group>: the outer N
      each handle one level's header/subtotal, and the innermost handles the detail rows. This file has two levels, so
      there are three nested <group> in all: salesperson, customer, month (the detail row itself is the monthly total,
      so no other unique key is needed).

      Headers/subtotals are decided as a "chain": when an outer key changes, the inner level is treated as changed too and
      its header is printed again (even if the customer code happens to repeat; this is standard control-break report
      behavior, not a bug). Subtotals go the other way: the inner level prints first (customer subtotal first, then the salesperson
      subtotal), matching how a nested report folds from the inside out.

      ORDER BY must follow the grouping levels (salesperson, then customer, then month). This matters as much with two
      levels as with one, but the risk doubles — if any level is not sorted, that level's
      header is split into several repeated pieces.

      [Fixed] All FORMAT('%d', ...) were changed to FORMAT('%.0f', ...). xy.amt comes
      from the SQL sum() aggregate, and many database drivers return SUM(integer column) as
      Double/Currency, no longer an integer; the FORMAT function does branch on the actual type,
      but the format string is passed to Format() unchanged — '%d' is an integer-only
      format code, and feeding it a floating-point value makes Format() itself throw (Numeric error).
      '%.0f' accepts both integers and floats and still shows no decimals. This problem
      did not show up in crosstab.wml because its values first go through CrossGrid's string
      grid and back through VAL(), being implicitly converted to integers on the way; report.wml reads
      xy.amt directly, without that conversion, so the problem shows up there.
    -->

    <dbquery id="xy">
      <![CDATA[
select
  year(s.sdate)                          yy,
  date_format(s.sdate, '%Y%m')           ym,
  s.Eno                                  eno,
  s.Cno                                  cust,
  sum(coalesce(s.Amount, 0))             amt
from sh s
where s.sdate >= '2026-01-01' and s.sdate < '2026-10-01'
group by
  date_format(s.sdate, '%Y%m'),
  s.Eno,
  s.Cno
order by yy, s.Eno, cust, ym
      ]]>
    </dbquery>

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="wf-head">
        <div class="wf-badge">WapForm for Web · Example</div>
        <div class="wf-title">Grouped Sales Report</div>
        <div class="wf-sub">2026/01 ~ 2026/09, grouped in two levels by salesperson/customer, with amounts by month and subtotals and totals at every level.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <report dataset="xy">

        <setvar name="No" value="0"/>
        <!-- EmpSubtotal: subtotal for the current salesperson, reset once for each new salesperson -->
        <setvar name="EmpSubtotal" value="0.0"/>
        <!-- CustSubtotal: subtotal for the current customer, reset once for each new customer -->
        <setvar name="CustSubtotal" value="0.0"/>
        <!-- Grand: total for all salespeople, reset only this once -->
        <setvar name="Grand" value="0.0"/>

        <table class="rpt-table">
          <tr>
            <th>#</th>
            <th>Month</th>
            <th>Amount</th>
          </tr>

          <group change="xy.eno">

            <!-- first-level header: salesperson -->
            <tr class="rpt-group-row">
              <td colspan="3">Salesperson: $(xy.eno)</td>
            </tr>
            <setvar name="EmpSubtotal" value="0"/>

            <group change="xy.cust">

              <!-- second-level header: customer -->
              <tr class="rpt-subgroup-row">
                <td colspan="3">Customer: $(xy.cust)</td>
              </tr>
              <setvar name="CustSubtotal" value="0"/>

              <group change="xy.ym">
                <setvar name="No" value="No+1"/>
                <tr>
                  <td>$(No)</td>
                  <td>$(xy.ym)</td>
                  <td>$(FORMAT('%.0f',xy.amt))</td>
                </tr>
                <setvar name="CustSubtotal" value="CustSubtotal + xy.amt"/>
                <setvar name="EmpSubtotal" value="EmpSubtotal + xy.amt"/>
                <setvar name="Grand" value="Grand + xy.amt"/>
              </group>

              <!-- second-level subtotal: customer subtotal, printed once before the customer changes -->
              <tr class="rpt-subtotal-row">
                <td colspan="2">Customer subtotal</td>
                <td>$(FORMAT('%.0f',CustSubtotal))</td>
              </tr>

            </group>

            <!-- first-level subtotal: salesperson subtotal, printed once before the salesperson changes -->
            <tr class="rpt-empsubtotal-row">
              <td colspan="2">Salesperson subtotal</td>
              <td>$(FORMAT('%.0f',EmpSubtotal))</td>
            </tr>

          </group>

          <tr class="rpt-grand-row">
            <td colspan="2">Total</td>
            <td>$(FORMAT('%.0f',Grand))</td>
          </tr>

        </table>
      </report>
    </div>

    <div class="narrow">
      <div class="foot">
        Layout comes from <code>wapform.htm</code>; the data and grouping logic are defined in <code>report.wml</code>.<br/>
        Data source <code>sh</code> (sales order headers), grouped in two levels by salesperson → customer and summarized by month, period 2026/01 ~ 2026/09,
        with exactly the same filters as the <a href="crosstab.wml">cross analysis</a>.<br/>
        It uses <b>the same query</b> as the <a href="crosstab.wml">cross analysis</a>, presented differently — one is a grouped list report,
        the other a row x column cross matrix; both use the raw customer code, so the amounts can be compared cell by cell.<br/>
        More examples: <a href="multiupload.wml">File Upload</a>, <a href="chart.wml">Chart Components</a>,
        <a href="crosstab.wml">Sales Cross Analysis</a>, <a href="linepush.wml?push_userid=&amp;push_text=">LINE Push</a>, <a href="function.wml">Function Self-Test</a>.
      </div>
    </div>

  </card>

</wml>
